 "questions": [
  {
   "id": 233223,
   "is_answer_order_important": true,
   "stimulus_html": "",
   "stem_html": "<p><img src=\"exercises/media/6ce251868b6f4338.jpg\" alt=\"A table of with the codon and what amino acid it codes for, which correlate as follows: GCA - alanine, AAG - lysine, GUU - valine, AAU -asparagine, UGC - cysteine, UCG and UCU - serine, UUA - Leucine, and UAA - stop.\"></p>\n\n<p>You are given three mRNA sequences:</p>\n\n<ol>\n  <li>5’-UCG-GCA- AAU-UUA -GUU-3’</li>\n  <li>5’-UCU-GCA- AAU-UUA -GUU-3’</li>\n  <li>5’-UCU-GCA- AAU-UAA -GUU-3’</li>\n</ol>\n\n<p>Using the peptide encoded by each of the above, compare the three peptides obtained. How are peptides 2 and 3 different from 1? What would be the consequence for the cell in each case?</p>\n",
   "answers": [
    {
     "id": 723038,
     "content_html": "There is a silent mutation in peptide 2 and peptide 3 has a stop codon due to mutation."
    },
    {
     "id": 723037,
     "content_html": "There is a silent mutation in peptide 3 and peptide 2 has a stop codon due to mutation."
    },
    {
     "id": 723036,
     "content_html": "There is a different amino acid in peptide 2 and peptide 3 has a stop codon due to mutation."
    },
    {
     "id": 723035,
     "content_html": "There isn’t a mutation in peptide 2 and peptide 3 has a stop codon due to mutation."
    }
   ],
